From: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri@mythread.it>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] b43: run the frame of the PAPD calibration on radio 2057 rev 8

b43_nphy_papd_cal() writes the tables the PAPD engine reads and stops
there. What the vendor driver does around them is hold the receive reset
off, take the transmit chain of one core and point it at the receive chain
through the coupler, put a tone into it, and hand all of it back
afterwards. That is the frame the gain index search runs inside, and
without it the search would have nothing to measure.

Add the frame, which is brcmsmac's wlc_phy_papd_cal_setup_nphy() and
wlc_phy_papd_cal_cleanup_nphy() for N-PHY rev 7 and up, together with the
parts of wlc_phy_a4() itself that surround them: the receive reset saved
and cleared between the scalar tables and the epsilon tables and written
back at the end, the baseband multipliers saved before the per core loop,
the comparison shift switched off again after it, and the transmit digital
filter the calibration runs behind - row 3 of tbl_tx_filter_coef_rev4
while it runs, the filters the PHY init programmed once it is over.

Neither the filter nor the cleanup is separable from the setup. Leaving
the calibration's filter in place outside the calibration is worse than
not touching it, and a setup with no cleanup leaves the RF control
overrides, the AFE overrides and the coupler forced on.

The epsilon tables now go out one cell at a time instead of as a bulk of
64. brcmsmac and the hardware's own driver both do it that way; a bulk
leaves the same tables behind and costs one table address setup instead of
64, but it also leans on the table address incrementing by itself, which
nothing here has ever tested.

Only the 2 GHz path is here. The 5 GHz coupler takes different constants
and no capture of it exists, so the calibration returns before the frame
outside 2 GHz, and the existing revision test keeps every other radio on
what it did before.

What measures the tone and writes the epsilon table, brcmsmac's
wlc_phy_a3_nphy() and wlc_phy_a2_nphy(), is still missing, so between the
setup and the cleanup nothing happens yet and the engine keeps running on
the cleared tables. The calibration is a no-op with a cost, and it is
worth landing in this shape because it is the part that can be checked
against a capture without any new arithmetic.

Checked as one contiguous region rather than per function, because the
harness's read plans are positional and only serve the value the hardware
gave if the port makes the same reads in the same order. Of the 2486
operations wl 6.30.102.7 issues between the scalar table and the filters
at the end, 1830 are in blocks common to both sequences, in the same order
and with the same values read back, the first of them 847 long. The two
gaps that are left, 349 and 276 operations, are the gain index search for
the two cores.

The same shape comes out of a second, independent capture, the one of a
cold init: the same 1830 operations in blocks, the same first block of 847,
and gaps of 920 and 930 where that one runs a full gain search instead of a
soft one.

Two gaps are values and not sequences, and both are on registers nothing
writes: the transmit/receive coupling attenuation of the two cores, which
the vendor reads as 0xaa and the port as a zero. That value is the chip's
own default - it is in neither driver's radio table, and the vendor never
writes it either - so the only place it exists is the capture. The third is
the read of radio 0x81 described above.

One operation of the vendor's has no counterpart: a read of radio 0x81,
TR2G_CONFIG1_CORE0_NU, which it makes once per core inside the setup,
between the four AFE reads and the four modifications of the same
registers. The register is written once per init, with 1, and read four
times in 70796 records, always in that one place; brcmsmac never touches
it. Whether the value is used for anything is not visible in a capture, so
nothing here reads it.

Verified: records #10966-#13918, from
"TBL.WR   id=0x0020 off=0x0000 len=64"
Link: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/phy/phy_n.c?id=848acc8ffe1b#n25108
Link: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/phy/phy_n.c?id=848acc8ffe1b#n24242
Link: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/phy/phy_n.c?id=848acc8ffe1b#n24496
Link: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/phy/phy_n.c?id=848acc8ffe1b#n14735
Link: https://github.com/aleferri/b43-6362-wip/blob/04e05f5/router-data/dsl-3580l/opinit-ch1-ch6-bw20.decoded
Link: https://github.com/aleferri/b43-6362-wip/blob/04e05f5/test/phase_compare.py, region papd-cal

While the frame is going in, name the two registers the channel measurement
reads. On N-PHY rev 8 the addresses 0x1C9 and 0x1CA are not the GPIO output
latches the existing names say: the capture reads them 3400 times in 70796
records and writes them never, and what comes back is a pair of
sign-extended 6 bit fields with one of them noise around zero. The old
names stay for the revisions that do use them as output latches; the new
ones sit beside them.

The measurement itself, 100 samples of one core then 100 of the other
inside the same eight register save and restore that frames
b43_nphy_poll_rssi(), is here but has no caller, and that is a measured
result rather than an omission: putting it at the end of the channel switch
costs 74 operations of the window this patch is verified against, because
the vendor driver does not take the measurement while an interface comes
up. It takes it on the channel switches of a scan. So it belongs above the
PHY, and this is not the patch that finds out where.

Signed-off-by: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri@mythread.it>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.h
@@ -604,6 +604,15 @@
 #define B43_NPHY_DSSSCCK_GAINSL			B43_PHY_N(0x1C8) /* DSSS/CCK gain settle length */
 #define B43_NPHY_GPIO_LOOUT			B43_PHY_N(0x1C9) /* GPIO low out */
 #define B43_NPHY_GPIO_HIOUT			B43_PHY_N(0x1CA) /* GPIO high out */
+/* The same two addresses carry a per-core measurement readback on N-PHY rev 8,
+ * not an output latch: in an MMIO capture of wl 6.30.102.7 on a BCM6362 they are
+ * read 3400 times in 70796 records and written zero times, and what comes back
+ * is a pair of sign-extended 6 bit fields, one of them noise around zero. The
+ * RSSI readback of the calibration is elsewhere and is used as well, 0x219 and
+ * 0x21A below, 648 reads in the same capture.
+ */
+#define B43_NPHY_REV8_MEAS_C1			B43_PHY_N(0x1C9) /* Measurement core 1 */
+#define B43_NPHY_REV8_MEAS_C2			B43_PHY_N(0x1CA) /* Measurement core 2 */
 #define B43_NPHY_CRS_CHECK			B43_PHY_N(0x1CB) /* CRS check */
 #define B43_NPHY_ML_LOGSS_RAT			B43_PHY_N(0x1CC) /* ML/logss ratio */
 #define B43_NPHY_DUPSCALE			B43_PHY_N(0x1CD) /* Dup scale */
@@ -937,6 +946,11 @@ struct b43_phy_n_pwr_ctl_info {
 };
 
 struct b43_phy_n {
+	/* The per-core measurement the channel switch takes, four sign-extended
+	 * 6 bit fields packed as the RSSI poll packs them. What the vendor
+	 * driver computes from it is not known, so nothing acts on it yet.
+	 */
+	s32 chan_meas;
 	u8 antsel_type;
 	u8 cal_orig_pwr_idx[2];
 	u8 measure_hold;
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
@@ -51,6 +51,21 @@ struct nphy_iq_est {
 	u32 q1_pwr;
 };
 
+/* What the PAPD calibration takes over for its own duration and has to put
+ * back afterwards.
+ */
+struct nphy_papd_restore_state {
+	u16 afectrl[2];
+	u16 afeoverride[2];
+	/* The vendor driver saves the transmit/receive coupling power-up and
+	 * then powers it down unconditionally, so only the attenuation is ever
+	 * put back. Kept to make the pair of reads visible.
+	 */
+	u16 pwrup[2];
+	u16 atten[2];
+	u16 bbmult;
+};
+
 enum b43_nphy_rf_sequence {
 	B43_RFSEQ_RX2TX,
 	B43_RFSEQ_TX2RX,
@@ -6079,14 +6095,197 @@ static void b43_nphy_superswitch_init(struct b43_wldev *dev, bool init)
  * lookup with its constants, and the epsilon tables cleared. Without this the
  * engine runs on whatever the tables happened to contain.
  */
-static void b43_nphy_papd_tables_init(struct b43_wldev *dev)
+static void b43_nphy_papd_scalar_tables(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 {
-	static const u32 zero[64] = { 0 };
-
 	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB32(32, 0), 64, b43_ntab_papd_scaltbl);
 	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB32(34, 0), 64, b43_ntab_papd_scaltbl);
-	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB32(31, 0), 64, zero);
-	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB32(33, 0), 64, zero);
+}
+
+/* The two epsilon tables, cleared one cell at a time, one core after the other,
+ * the way brcmsmac and the hardware's own driver do it. A bulk write leaves the
+ * same tables behind for one table address setup instead of 64, but it leans on
+ * the address incrementing by itself, which nothing here has tested.
+ */
+static void b43_nphy_papd_epsilon_tables_clear(struct b43_wldev *dev)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 64; i++)
+		b43_ntab_write(dev, B43_NTAB32(31, i), 0);
+	for (i = 0; i < 64; i++)
+		b43_ntab_write(dev, B43_NTAB32(33, i), 0);
+}
+
+/* The registers the PAPD calibration drives one core at a time. The first four
+ * are PHY registers, the last two radio ones, and the two address spaces are
+ * indexed by the same core number.
+ */
+static const struct {
+	u16 afectl;
+	u16 afeover;
+	u16 papd_en;
+	u16 cal_shifts;
+	u16 pwrup;
+	u16 atten;
+} b43_nphy_papd_regs[2] = {
+	{ B43_NPHY_AFECTL_C1, B43_NPHY_AFECTL_OVER1,
+	  B43_NPHY_PAPD_EN0, B43_NPHY_PAPD_CAL_SHIFTS0,
+	  R2057_TX0_TXRXCOUPLE_2G_PWRUP, R2057_TX0_TXRXCOUPLE_2G_ATTEN },
+	{ B43_NPHY_AFECTL_C2, B43_NPHY_AFECTL_OVER,
+	  B43_NPHY_PAPD_EN1, B43_NPHY_PAPD_CAL_SHIFTS1,
+	  R2057_TX1_TXRXCOUPLE_2G_PWRUP, R2057_TX1_TXRXCOUPLE_2G_ATTEN },
+};
+
+/* The baseband multipliers the calibration works on: table 15 offsets 87 and
+ * 95. brcmsmac's wlc_phy_ipa_get_bbmult_nphy() and its set counterpart.
+ */
+static u16 b43_nphy_ipa_get_bbmult(struct b43_wldev *dev)
+{
+	return b43_ntab_read(dev, B43_NTAB16(15, 87));
+}
+
+static void b43_nphy_ipa_set_bbmult(struct b43_wldev *dev, u8 m0, u8 m1)
+{
+	u16 m0m1 = (m0 << 8) | m1;
+
+	b43_ntab_write(dev, B43_NTAB16(15, 87), m0m1);
+	b43_ntab_write(dev, B43_NTAB16(15, 95), m0m1);
+}
+
+/* The transmit digital filter the PAPD calibration runs behind, which is not
+ * the one the PHY init leaves in place: row 3 of tbl_tx_filter_coef_rev4 at
+ * 20 MHz, row 4 at 40 MHz. brcmsmac's
+ * wlc_phy_ipa_restore_tx_digi_filts_nphy(). What the init programs goes back
+ * once the calibration is done, which is b43_nphy_int_pa_set_tx_dig_filters().
+ */
+static void b43_nphy_int_pa_restore_tx_dig_filters(struct b43_wldev *dev)
+{
+	if (b43_is_40mhz(dev))
+		b43_nphy_pa_set_tx_dig_filter(dev, 0x195,
+					      tbl_tx_filter_coef_rev4[4]);
+	else
+		b43_nphy_pa_set_tx_dig_filter(dev, 0x186,
+					      tbl_tx_filter_coef_rev4[3]);
+}
+
+/* Point the transmit chain of one core at the receive chain through the coupler
+ * and put a tone into it, which is the measurement the PAPD gain search runs
+ * on, keeping what has to be handed back. brcmsmac's
+ * wlc_phy_papd_cal_setup_nphy(), N-PHY rev 7 and up, 2 GHz.
+ */
+static void b43_nphy_papd_cal_setup(struct b43_wldev *dev,
+				    struct nphy_papd_restore_state *state,
+				    u8 core)
+{
+	u8 off_core = core ^ 1;
+
+	/* The low pass filter bandwidth stays where the channel put it. */
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x80, b43_nphy_read_lpf_ctl(dev, 0),
+				      0, false, 1);
+	/* Radio 2057 rev 7 and 8 want no transmit mixer gain of their own. */
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x800, 0, 1 << core, false, 0);
+
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_one_to_many(dev, N_RF_CTL_OVER_CMD_TX_PU, 1,
+					     1 << core, false);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_one_to_many(dev, N_RF_CTL_OVER_CMD_TX_PU, 0,
+					     1 << off_core, false);
+
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x8, 0, 0x3, false, 0);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x4, 1, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x1, 0, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x2, 1, 1 << core, false, 2);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x100, 0, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x200, 1, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x400, 0, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x8, 1, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x20, 0, 1 << core, false, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x10, 0, 1 << core, false, 1);
+
+	state->afectrl[core] =
+		b43_phy_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].afectl);
+	state->afeoverride[core] =
+		b43_phy_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].afeover);
+	state->afectrl[off_core] =
+		b43_phy_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].afectl);
+	state->afeoverride[off_core] =
+		b43_phy_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].afeover);
+
+	/* The core being calibrated drives the digital to analog converter
+	 * from the calibration, the other one keeps its own value.
+	 */
+	b43_phy_mask(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].afectl, ~0x4);
+	b43_phy_set(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].afeover, 0x4);
+	b43_phy_set(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].afectl, 0x4);
+	b43_phy_set(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].afeover, 0x4);
+
+	state->pwrup[core] =
+		b43_radio_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].pwrup);
+	state->atten[core] =
+		b43_radio_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].atten);
+	state->pwrup[off_core] =
+		b43_radio_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].pwrup);
+	state->atten[off_core] =
+		b43_radio_read(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].atten);
+
+	b43_radio_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].pwrup, 0xC);
+	b43_radio_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].atten, 0xF0);
+	b43_radio_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].pwrup, 0x0);
+	b43_radio_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].atten, 0xFF);
+
+	b43_nphy_tx_tone(dev, 4000, 181, false, false, false);
+
+	b43_phy_set(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].papd_en, 0x1);
+	b43_phy_set(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].cal_shifts, 0x2000);
+	b43_phy_mask(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].papd_en, ~0x1);
+	b43_phy_mask(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[off_core].cal_shifts, ~0x2000);
+}
+
+/* Give the PHY and the radio back what b43_nphy_papd_cal_setup() took, in the
+ * order brcmsmac's wlc_phy_papd_cal_cleanup_nphy() does. The value argument of
+ * the overrides is ignored once they are being switched off; it is kept as
+ * brcmsmac passes it so the two sequences read the same.
+ */
+static void b43_nphy_papd_cal_cleanup(struct b43_wldev *dev,
+				      struct nphy_papd_restore_state *state)
+{
+	u8 core;
+
+	b43_nphy_stop_playback(dev);
+
+	for (core = 0; core < 2; core++) {
+		b43_radio_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].pwrup, 0);
+		b43_radio_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].atten,
+				state->atten[core]);
+	}
+
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x4, 0, 0x3, true, 0);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x2, 0, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x1, 0, 0x3, true, 2);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x4, 0, 0x3, true, 2);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x800, 1, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x8, 0, 0x3, true, 0);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x800, 0, 0x3, true, 0);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x1000, 0, 0x3, true, 0);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x4, 1, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x1, 0, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x2, 1, 0x3, true, 2);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x100, 0, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x200, 1, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x400, 0, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x8, 1, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x20, 0, 0x3, true, 1);
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x10, 0, 0x3, true, 1);
+
+	for (core = 0; core < 2; core++) {
+		b43_phy_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].afectl,
+			      state->afectrl[core]);
+		b43_phy_write(dev, b43_nphy_papd_regs[core].afeover,
+			      state->afeoverride[core]);
+	}
+
+	b43_nphy_ipa_set_bbmult(dev, state->bbmult >> 8, state->bbmult & 0xFF);
+
+	b43_nphy_rf_ctl_override_rev7(dev, 0x80, 0, 0, true, 1);
 }
 
 /* The PAPD calibration, brcmsmac's wlc_phy_a4(). Only the part that does not
@@ -6099,13 +6298,56 @@ static void b43_nphy_papd_tables_init(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 static void b43_nphy_papd_cal(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 {
 	struct b43_phy *phy = &dev->phy;
+	struct nphy_papd_restore_state state;
+	u16 bbcfg;
+	u8 core;
 
 	if (!b43_nphy_ipa(dev))
 		return;
 
 	/* Only radio 2057 rev 8 is known to want these constants. */
-	if (phy->rev == 8 && phy->radio_rev == 8)
-		b43_nphy_papd_tables_init(dev);
+	if (phy->rev != 8 || phy->radio_rev != 8)
+		return;
+
+	b43_nphy_papd_scalar_tables(dev);
+
+	/* The receive reset stays off while the calibration transmits into its
+	 * own receive chain, and whatever it was goes back at the end.
+	 */
+	bbcfg = b43_phy_read(dev, B43_NPHY_BBCFG);
+	b43_phy_mask(dev, B43_NPHY_BBCFG, ~B43_NPHY_BBCFG_RSTRX & 0xFFFF);
+
+	b43_nphy_papd_epsilon_tables_clear(dev);
+
+	/* Below here is the 2 GHz path only, which is the one the capture this
+	 * was built from exercises. The 5 GHz coupler constants differ and are
+	 * unverified.
+	 */
+	if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) != NL80211_BAND_2GHZ)
+		return;
+
+	b43_nphy_int_pa_restore_tx_dig_filters(dev);
+
+	state.bbmult = b43_nphy_ipa_get_bbmult(dev);
+
+	for (core = 0; core < 2; core++) {
+		b43_nphy_papd_cal_setup(dev, &state, core);
+		/* What measures the tone and writes the epsilon table,
+		 * brcmsmac's wlc_phy_a3_nphy() and wlc_phy_a2_nphy(), is not
+		 * here yet: the engine keeps running on the cleared tables.
+		 */
+		b43_nphy_papd_cal_cleanup(dev, &state);
+	}
+
+	/* The comparison shift the setup switched on for the core it was
+	 * calibrating goes off again: what stays on outside the calibration has
+	 * to be the driver's state, not the calibration's.
+	 */
+	b43_phy_mask(dev, B43_NPHY_PAPD_CAL_SHIFTS0, ~0x2000);
+	b43_phy_mask(dev, B43_NPHY_PAPD_CAL_SHIFTS1, ~0x2000);
+
+	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_BBCFG, bbcfg);
+	b43_nphy_int_pa_set_tx_dig_filters(dev);
 }
 
 /* The offset the PAPD engine applies to the epsilon table. brcmsmac computes it
@@ -6823,6 +7065,63 @@ static void b43_nphy_op_switch_analog(struct b43_wldev *dev, bool on)
 	}
 }
 
+/* The measurement the vendor driver takes on every channel it visits, on N-PHY
+ * rev 8: 100 samples of one core and then 100 of the other, inside the same
+ * eight register save and restore that frames b43_nphy_poll_rssi(). It is not
+ * that function - that one interleaves the two cores and reads the calibration's
+ * RSSI registers, which this radio uses too, elsewhere.
+ *
+ * The result goes into nphy->chan_meas and nothing reads it. That is deliberate:
+ * the capture says what is measured and how, and does not say what is computed
+ * from it, so inventing a consumer would be worse than leaving the value there
+ * for the code that identifies it.
+ *
+ * NOTHING CALLS THIS YET, and the call site is a measured question rather than an
+ * open one. Calling it at the end of the channel switch costs 74 operations of
+ * the window that compares an interface coming up against the capture, because
+ * the vendor driver does not take this measurement while bringing the interface
+ * up: it takes it on the channel switches of a scan, after the point that window
+ * ends. So it is not part of switch_channel, and where it does belong is above
+ * the PHY, in the code that drives a scan dwell.
+ */
+static void b43_nphy_rev8_chan_meas(struct b43_wldev *dev)
+{
+	struct b43_phy_n *nphy = dev->phy.n;
+	static const u16 save_regs[] = {
+		B43_NPHY_AFECTL_OVER1, B43_NPHY_AFECTL_OVER,
+		B43_NPHY_AFECTL_C1, B43_NPHY_AFECTL_C2,
+		B43_NPHY_TXF_40CO_B1S0, B43_NPHY_TXF_40CO_B32S1,
+		B43_NPHY_RFCTL_LUT_TRSW_UP1, B43_NPHY_RFCTL_LUT_TRSW_UP2,
+	};
+	static const u16 meas_regs[] = {
+		B43_NPHY_REV8_MEAS_C1, B43_NPHY_REV8_MEAS_C2,
+	};
+	u16 saved[ARRAY_SIZE(save_regs)];
+	s32 buf[4] = { 0 };
+	unsigned int i, core;
+	u16 val;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(save_regs); i++)
+		saved[i] = b43_phy_read(dev, save_regs[i]);
+
+	b43_nphy_rssi_select(dev, 5, N_RSSI_NB);
+
+	for (core = 0; core < ARRAY_SIZE(meas_regs); core++) {
+		for (i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
+			val = b43_phy_read(dev, meas_regs[core]);
+			buf[2 * core] += ((s8)((val & 0x3F) << 2)) >> 2;
+			buf[2 * core + 1] +=
+				((s8)(((val >> 8) & 0x3F) << 2)) >> 2;
+		}
+	}
+
+	nphy->chan_meas = (buf[0] & 0xFF) << 24 | (buf[1] & 0xFF) << 16 |
+			  (buf[2] & 0xFF) << 8 | (buf[3] & 0xFF);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(save_regs); i++)
+		b43_phy_write(dev, save_regs[i], saved[i]);
+}
